```python
def primes_sieve(limit):
    limitn = limit+1
    primes = dict()
    for ind, val in enumerate([True] * limitn): primes[ind] = val

    primes[0] = primes[1] = False

    for ind, val in enumerate(primes):
        if val is True:
            primes[ind*2::ind] = [False] * (((limit - ind)//ind) - 1)
    return [ind for ind, val in enumerate(primes) if val is True]

def divide_by_r(n, r):
    return n % r == 0

def is_palindrome(n):
    return str(n) == str(n)[::-1]

def is_even(n):
    return n % 2 == 0

def check_prime_palindrome(n, p, q, r):
    prime_list = [i for i in primes_sieve(q) if i >= p and len(str(i)) == n]
    result = []
    for prime in prime_list:
        if is_palindrome(prime) and divide_by_r(prime, r) and not is_even(prime):
            result.append(prime)
    return result
```

For the temporal efficiency, the time complexity of this code is primarily affected by the "primes_sieve" function which generates all prime numbers up to q. This function operates with a time complexity of O(n log(log n)), where n equals to q in this context. In the worst case, the complexity is O(q log(log q)), plus additional overhead for checking palindromes and divisibility by r. As such, for large inputs of q, the processing time may increase considerably, but the running time will always be within a reasonable limit for any practical size of q as the sieve of Eratosthenes is one of the most efficient ways to find all primes smaller than n."}]

